<article class="env-proposition" id="0566">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0566">Proposition <span data-tag="0566">3.5.9.8</span>.</a> Let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a Kan fibration between Kan complexes and let $n$ be an integer. Then $f$ is $n$-truncated (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="055Z" href="/tag/055Z">3.5.9.1</a>) if and only if, for each vertex $y \in Y$, the Kan complex $X_{y} = \{ y\}  \times _{Y} X$ is $n$-truncated (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="054P" href="/tag/054P">3.5.7.1</a>). </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      For $n \geq 0$, this follows from Corollary <a data-tag="050Z" href="/tag/050Z">3.2.6.8</a>. This extends to the case $n = -1$ by virtue of Variant <a data-tag="0510" href="/tag/0510">3.2.6.9</a>, and to the case $n \leq -2$ by virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="00WP" href="/tag/00WP">3.2.6.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
